<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hengist and Horsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Hengest and Horsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hengist and Horsa are legendary brothers said to have led the first Germanic settlers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the 5th century. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, they came around 449 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of the British ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> and then rebelled against him; Horsa was killed in battle, and Hengist founded the kingdom of Kent. Most historians treat the story as a foundation legend rather than history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa.jpg" alt="Hengist and Horsa" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Edward Parrott · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hengist_and_Horsa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
